\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Reflections, halves and the positive-time domain}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{Y} be a finite spatial torus with coordinate periods at
least three, or a point. Put
#{X=Y\times\mathbb Z/(2M)}, where #{M\ge2}; the second coordinate
is the chosen time direction. The nearest-neighbor graph joins sites
differing by one coordinate step, with each undirected edge counted
once. Time indices below are taken modulo #{2M}. Define the
\newvocab{site reflection} and \newvocab{link reflection} by
##{\theta_s(y,t)=(y,-t),\qquad
   \theta_\ell(y,t)=(y,1-t).}
Their positive halves and fixed sets are
##{\begin{aligned}
X_+^s&=Y\times\{0,\ldots,M\},&
X_0^s&=Y\times\{0,M\},\\
X_+^\ell&=Y\times\{1,\ldots,M\},&
X_0^\ell&=\varnothing.
\end{aligned}}
In either case set #{X_-=\theta X_+}. Then
#{X_+\cup X_-=X} and #{X_+\cap X_-=X_0}; sites in #{X_0}
are fixed individually.}

\texfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  cell/.style={draw,minimum width=7mm,minimum height=7mm,
    inner sep=1pt,font=\small},
  label/.style={font=\small,anchor=west}
]
\node[label] at (-0.35,0.85) {Site reflection: $t\mapsto-t$};
\foreach \t/\s in {0/*,1/+,2/+,3/+,4/*,5/-,6/-,7/-}{
  \node[cell] at (0.75*\t,0) {$\t^{\s}$};
}
\node[label] at (-0.35,-0.95) {Link reflection: $t\mapsto1-t$};
\foreach \t/\s in {0/-,1/+,2/+,3/+,4/+,5/-,6/-,7/-}{
  \node[cell] at (0.75*\t,-1.8) {$\t^{\s}$};
}
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The diagram shows one time circle of length eight. A plus or
minus marks an interior site of that half; a star marks a fixed
site shared by both halves. Time #{7} is adjacent to time #{0}.
The boxes display membership in the halves, not separate field copies.}

\p{For a real scalar configuration #{\phi\in\mathbb R^X}, write
#{(\theta\phi)_v=\phi_{\theta v}}. On complex observables define
##{(\Theta f)(\phi)=\overline{f(\theta\phi)}.}
Thus #{\Theta} is an antilinear multiplicative involution.
Let #{\mathcal A_+} be the algebra of bounded complex measurable
functions depending only on #{\phi|_{X_+}}. Let #{\mu} be a
probability measure on #{\mathbb R^X}. Reflection invariance means
#{\theta_*\mu=\mu}. In addition, \newvocab{reflection positivity}
means
##{B_\mu(f,f):=\int(\Theta f)f\,d\mu\ge0
 \qquad(f\in\mathcal A_+).}
The inequality includes the assertion that the integral is real.
Under reflection invariance, #{B_\mu(f,g)=\int(\Theta f)g\,d\mu}
is Hermitian and conjugate-linear in its first argument. These are
the complex-observable conventions corresponding to
\citet{Definition 10.5, p. 441}{friedli2017statistical}.}

\p{The larger positive-time domain is
#{\mathcal H_+=L^2(\mathbb R^X,\mu;\mathbb C)}
restricted to functions measurable in the coordinates of #{X_+}.
It is a closed vector subspace, generally not an algebra under
pointwise multiplication. Reflection invariance gives
##{|B_\mu(f,g)|\le\|f\|_{L^2(\mu)}\|g\|_{L^2(\mu)}.}
Truncating the values of a function shows that #{\mathcal A_+}
is dense in #{\mathcal H_+}. Consequently positivity on
#{\mathcal A_+} extends to this domain. For a reflection-positive
law, the quotient by
#{\mathcal N=\{f:B_\mu(f,f)=0\}}, with the induced pairing,
has a Hilbert-space completion. This definition supplies no
translation operator on that quotient.}
